Triumph of right-wing populists in Austria, together have 29 per cent
Significant weakening of both parties of the current Grand Coalition so far and a significant reinforcement of right-wing populist parties brought Sunday's early parliamentary elections in Austria.
The Austrian People's Party (ÖVP) suffered particularly heavy casualties, where the position of the current chairman Wilhelm Molterer is seriously shaken.
Conversely, the electoral leader of the Confederation for the Future of Austria (BZÖ), Carnite Governor Jörg Haider is preparing a triumphant return to national politics.
According to preliminary results, it remains the most posting party in Social-Democracy (SPÖ) with 29.8 per cent of the poll, losing 5.5 per cent of the vote.
ÖVP lost even worse, losing almost nine percent of the vote with 25.6 percent.
These are the worst results of both of these big parties in Austrian post-war history and, especially among the People's Party, which provoked an early-elect, literally a disastrous result.
Even at the beginning of July, when ÖVP came out of the coalition, the People's Party had a considerable head start in their preferences over the SPÖ.
Voters, however, apparently punished them for letting the government crash.
Already in the first reactions to the results there was speculation about the possible resignation of the party's chairman and the current Vice Chancellor Wilhelm Molterer.
Observers believe this could take place as early as Monday at an emergency meeting of the party's bureau.
Such a development would probably facilitate the path to the increasingly proflith-presumables from an electoral result, i.e. the restoration of a grand Coalition of SPÖ and ÖVP.
Due to the strengthening of the two right-wing populist parties (FPÖ), they tentatively gained 18 percent and BZÖ eleven percent of the votes - at the same time, the Social Democrats expressed concern for a repetition in 1999, when the People's Party argued with populists (at that time it was still ununispersed, in 2005 it broke away from BZÖ) on a joint government that even provoked sanctions on the part of the European Union.
Haider, who has already stated that he is ready to cooperate with any party and presupposes a return to Vienna into national politics.
The Green Party got into the parliament, but it was slightly lost and plummeted among the Austrian political parties from the third to the fifth place.
None of the other parties clearly surpassed the four-percent threshold and did not make it to parliament.
