Travel Agencies Are Seeing Increased Pore-Pregnancies
It looks like winter is going to be good for German tourism entrepreneurs:
According to one study, the turnaround has risen significantly for next-month bookings compared to the previous year - even by more than 30 per cent for the winter season.
In spite of its weak economic growth, the appetite of the Germans remains unmistakable.
For bookings for the winter season, graduation agencies recorded an increase of 31 per cent compared to the same period of last year, according to the German Travel Association (Deutscher Reiseverband, DRV).
According to the GfK market research research survey, there are already increased reserves available for the autumn months.
The turnaround was about eleven percent higher compared to 2007, compared to 2007.
The basis for these results is GfK numbers, which annually evaluates booking data from 1200 German travel agencies every month and interpolates them into the overall market.
This result included bookings, which took place until the end of August 2008.
Due to the weak exchange rate of the U.S., travel to the U.S. is mainly demanded, the DRV said.
On these trips, the increment of reservations is about one-three.
In addition, Spain, Turkey and Greece, as well as domestic waters, are particularly popular targets for the Germans.
Above all, organized tours are on the course, DrV chairman Klaus Laepple said.
In view of rising energy expenditure and food, the costs here "it is easier for the client than for an individually organized holiday".
Laapple explained that consumers can, among other things, smash money by using discounts for early bookings or all-inclusive offers.
