Turkish Diplomatic Horizons Expansion Expansion
A long time before Turkey started trying to join the European Union, European powers longed to dig deep into the Turkish outskirts.
On the eastern bank of Bosphorus, perhaps only twenty steps in Asia, stands one of the finest monuments of this unsuccessful imperial ambition.
Hydarpasha railway station was designed by two German architects Otto Ritter and Helmut Con in neo-Renaissance style.
Tourist guides will tell you.
The building sits an astringe at the end of the platforms with a tower on every corner of the façade, indistinguishable from some provincial German town hall.
The stone is filthy.
The whole building slowly crumbles.
But as soon as a person gets inside the vaulted treasury, you can no longer have any doubt about the size of the enterprise whose westernmost gate was the station.
In August 1908 it was opened by an Anatolian-Baghdadian corporation in August 1908.
Germany, which took part in an imperire game late, intended to realize its ambitions to dominate the Middle East using this railway, which was supposed to lead into Damascus, Baghdad, and the territory, which today is Saudi Arabia.
But the German imperial ambitions collapsed during the First World War.
Their dormitories were invaded by Lawrence of Arabia and its non-schedules.
Relative Isolation from the Surrounding Territories Created by the Frozhiffon of the Cold Wrought-Woles is Away
The British and colonial forces advanced from Egypt, displaguing the Turks and their German allies from Palestine and Syria.
And there was also bad news for Hydarpasha.
In 1917 an ammunition train exploded its elaborate roof.
Turkish sources say it was sabotage.
Since then, it has gradually shrouded itself and history bypasses it.
But today this gateway to Anatolia and further east serves - at least symbolically - to accentuate the escalating horizons of Turkish diplomacy.
Ruling Justice and Development - AKP - created a completely new foreign policy for the country.
Relative issuance from the surrounding areas, formed by the frozen borders of the Cold War, is gone.
Now a new policy is being pursued - Turkey is getting involved.
It is noteworthy that the Turkish government has good ties with Israel-Syria, the Palestinian factions of Hamas and Fatah, with Iraq and Iran, and, of course, with the European Union and the United States.
In order to re-examine the parameters of this new policy, I boarded a night train in the Turkish capital Ankara, where I was supposed to meet one of AKP's leading experts on foreign policy, Turkish MP Suat Kiniklioglu.
The next morning - about ten-and-a-half an hour later - after an unlucky ride on the tracks commemorated, commemorating the old-world rhymes of the well-familiar documentary Night Mail, I arrived in the Turkish metropolis, designed as a new city for a secular state, which wants to turn its back to its Ottoman past.
However, AKP with its well-known Islamist roots represents a shift in the paradigms of the Turkish consensus.
It aroused acquiession in the west, especially in Washington.
The taxi brought me to the main tent AKP, an ultra-modern stone and metal building, deliberately erected in an unfamiliar prefabricated suburb, surrounded by half-understood departmental department stores, and power-trailers where middle managers in business suits - both men and women meet to make early morning shops.
Suat Kinniklioglu could easily be a centre-legged politician from almost every other European political party.
Jacket, tie, I'm sure Blackberry did, too.
"The party's foreign policy aims to reintegrate Turkey into its immediate vicinity.
The Cold War was an an anomaly," he told me.
Turkey has been cut off from its rescumble.
Today he has to look east and west at the same time.
It wasn't a choice.
It must've done both.
"But I'm sure there will be a moment," I asked, "when Turkey may have to make a decision?"
"Turkey," he said, "didn't want such a decision had to take place."
He then emphasized what almost everyone with whom I have spoken here said almost by heart: Turkish relations not only with the Middle East, but also with the Caucasus and around the Black Sea are as much for the benefit of Europe and the United States as Turkey.
With Turkey on board it seems that Europe as a whole can, metaphorically speaking, ride on these tracks east to Asia and the Middle East.
Back to Hydarpaša station, after another mostly waking night, I was thinking of the two German lords Herr Ritter and the Herr Con, who were watching this building stand up a hundred years ago.
How they would be surprised if they found that European ambitions in the Middle East can now be, largely, in Turkish hands.
And when I was waiting in the harbor for the ferry back through the Bosphorus, I remembered that in those days Turkey was called the "sick man of Europe".
So it seems that at least at that time there was not a lot of doubt which continent should be entitled to this fascinating country.
Sweatable Lives: Vernon Handley and William Woodruff
Mark Brown writes: As guildford music manager since 1962, Vernon "Tod" Handley (an obecologist, September 11) was in charge of the Guildford Philharmonic orchestra and choir.
As part of the district's musical plan, he founded an excellent choir of young Proteus a year later.
He was supposed to be under the age of 21, but at the time Proteus last sang in 1979, there were almost 30 of us - we simply refused to give up the congregation.
The name "Proteus" (mythological shape changer) represented the willingness and ability of the choir to embark on any music that was presented to it - whether madrigaly or Messiaen, either on its own or with an entire orchestra.
The musical adaptability created by such a young choir was often astonishing, always thrilling, all thanks to the inspiration of the only man and the enthusiasm for the music that has remained utterly immutable all the time.
How often Just Tod told us to "leave the music for ourselves".
He said: "The composer knows what he wants.
It's all in the music.
Sing it then! '
Its baton technique was absolutely clear and precise, but it was neither dictatorial nor flowery.
Wherever the musical standard was, he always lifted it up.
John Frayn Turner writes:
If some conductor was to be knighted, then it was Vernon Handley.
I knew him well in the two-decade, when he was conducting Guildford Philharmonic from 1962 until 1982.
Over the past 40 years, I've reviewed more than 100 of his gigs and I can honestly say I've never been misbehavior to any of them - and I've been inspired many times.
Tod Handley had such a great combination of size and personal modesty.
He hated the conductors who, according to his words, simply "pulled around the world one or two soaking stones".
We're going to miss a lot.
William Woodruff
Professor John Beckett writes:
Your necroologist William Woodruff (September 25) argues that "in 1946 he retired to economic history at Oxford and in 1950 he became a Hoiblon-Norman researcher... and at the same time he studied at BSc, MA and PhD".
This stems from Woodruff author's remembrance in Beyond Nab End, but it's not accurate.
From 1946 until 1953 he taught economic history at the University of Nottingham, and at that time he completed his dissertation on the British rubber industry, which was led by Professor J. D. Chambers, and subsequently published it in 1958 as the British Rubber Industry.
Truly he spent 1950-51 in America as a researcher at Hoiblon-Norman and summer 1952 at the University of Illinois, Harvard and New York.
In 1953 he finally left Nottingham and transferred to Illinois.
It is not known why Woodruff passed this part of his career.
