Hos Honda attacks its bestseller for the second time
In the month of November, Honda will start selling the second-generation Jazz.
If the forerunner of this single-spatial vehicle already possessed considerable space, it now offers an even more spacious interior.
Improved engines, which increase driving comfort and allow for greater fuel savings.
Evolution rather than a revolution.
That's the second-generation Honda Jazz.
Although from an aesphatical point of view the lines of the new Jazz are very similar to the previous model, there have been a lot of changes on this single-spatial car.
It is necessary to mention e.g. innovative design, a new platform, larger dimensions and new engines.
Even before we explore the new model, it should be noteworthy that the new Jazz is coming to the market six years after the launch of the first-generation.
Therefore, the first thing we notice is the fact that Honda needed a shorter period to rejuvenate this model than other manufacturers.
Although Jazz is a less well-familiearminated model in Europe, in other markets it has become a Phenomenon in sales.
This is the case of Japan, the country where Jazz was the best-selling car in 2002, and also during this year's 2008.
Nearly a million units have been shipwreck in Japan since the launch of Honda Jazz.
The second market became an old continent with about half a millennium units sold in six years.
Honda, however, aims to make the new Jazz increase sales in its category.
Thus, while in 2007 the Japanese company sold a total of 83,000 Honda Jazz, in 2009 the estimates include 100,000 cars, which would mean an increase of 17% on the European market.
Responsive to the Spanish market, Honda has sold a total of , 5,000 units of the Jazz model since 2002, a 6.7 per cent of Honda's total sales in Spain.
Regarding the new-season of Jazz, the management of the Spanish branch arguably awaits the sale of 2,000 units for the whole year.
It is the arrival of the new Jazz that will allow Honda to significantly alleviate the drop in sales in Spain.
The automaker's leadership thus de facto awaits the end of the year with a total of 22,000-sold units, which represents 4,000 fewer than in 2007 and a decrease of 16%.
In spite of this increase in sales, Honda increased its share of the Spanish market to 1.8%, compared to 1.6% from last year.
In 2010, this Japanese company expects it to reach 2%.
In order to achieve this share, Honda reliances on the success of its next new model.
It is Honda Insight, a five-speaker compact-sufficient compact-sufficient-size hybrid with a 4.37-metre-long range, from which the Japanese company promises a global sale of approximately 200 000 units a year.
When it comes to markets, according to the Japanese manufacturer's estimation, 50 per cent of Insight will sell in the U.S., while the remaining percentages will be distributed by the old continent and Japan.
Honda Insight sales are expected to begin in the first months of next year, as soon as it is presented to the public in Valencia in Valencia.
Compared to the first-regirth of the new Jazz, all dimensions have increased.
Its length increased up to 3.9 meters (five centimeters more), the width to 1.69 meters (two centimeters more) and the wheelbase to 2.5 meters, which means an extension of five centimeters.
On the contrary, the height did not change and is still 1.52 meters.
Enabling the dimensions were also to enlarge the inner space.
This advantage was achieved, on the one hand, the use of a new platform, on the other hand by storing a fuel tank in the middle of the car, which has already been used in the previous Honda Jazz.
The volume of the luggage compartment - which has a double-houlder system - has also increased up to 399 liters, 19 litres more than first-generation luggage capacity.
It should be noteworthy that these dimensions occur in a version with a 1.4 engine, as the luggage compartment volume at Honda Jazz 1.2 is 379 litres.
In addition to the external elements - such as rear LED lights or the possibility of equipping the glazied panoramic roof - it is easier to get access to the rear seats.
Doors leading to the second-seater of the seats allow an 80-degree opening angle compared to 67 on the first Jazz model.
For the second-generation Honda Jazz, there are also new engines.
From the beginning, this small model will be offered with two gasoline engines of 1.2 and 1.4 liters.
Both engines use i-VTEC technology, which allows for lower consumption, lower emissions of carbon dioxide and bring excellent improvements to the propulsion system at the same time with high torque at full engine speed.
The first 1.2-liter engine has an output of 90 hp at 6,000 rpm with a maximum torque of 114 Nm at 4,900 rpm.
This engine - which had 78 hp in the previous generation of the Jazz - has a consumption of 5.6 litres and an emissions of 125 grams of CO2.
Compared to the previous engine, which reached 83 hp for the previous generation the previous generation, it will now reach 100.
The 100-horse-powered Honda Jazz 1.4 has a combined 5.4-liter consumption and an emissions of 128 grams of CO2.
"Auto Bee" prevents collisions
For progress in the development of anti-accident-precision technologies, this time Nissan was inspired in nature, specifically beeves.
This Japanese company has created a robot that takes as a pattern of bee flying and thus prevents collisions.
Such is Bio-mimetic Car Robot Drive or BR23C, which represents the first step in collision prevention technologies.
Engineers of the Centre for Technical Advances of the Nissan Motor Co. robot have evolved, in cooperation with the Centre for Advance in Scientific and Technological Reconnaissance in Science and Technological Repetition.
According to Kazuiro Doi, the General Director of Public Relations for Technology, R&D and Brand Development, the BR23C is presented as an extension of Nissan's four safety-shield zones.
The robot is tasked to strengthen the ability to prevent rainfall in the cars of this brand.
During the flight, each bee creates its own oval-shaped personal space, which closely resembles a Nissan safety shield.
The most important, however, are folded bee eyes, which are able to be visible in all directions at an angle of more than 300 degrees, which the bee makes it possible to fly in its personal space without stopping.
Engineers, when creating this visual function, designed what they called a laser localizer (Laser Range Finder).
It detects obstetricities located up to a distance of two meters in a radius of 180 degrees in front of BR23C, calculates the distance of the object and sends a signal to an on-board microprocessor, which immediately converts it to a collision warning.
"The moment an obstacle is recorded, the robot imitates bee movements and immediately changes direction by rotating the wheels to an even even greater angle to prevent a collision.
The biggest difference compared to any existing system is the fact that all evasive manoeuvres are absolutely instinctive.
If that wasn’t the case, the robot wouldn’t be able to respond quickly enough to avoid obstacles,” explains Toshiyuki Andoh, Director of the Mobilní Laboratorial and Chief Engineer of this project.
Unlike bees, the robot cannot move upwards and down or diagonally, its movement is confined only to two dimensions and to the direction in which it is possible by rotating its wheels, but not in an infinite number of directions and possibilities as for bees.
In addition to accelerating and escalation, Nissan also took advantage of the rotation function in order to prevent a collision from colliding.
"Equipment only needs to process information every a few seconds and behave according to these data.
Doesn't require the preservation of information or logical intuition from previous manoeuvres, thanks to which it can function without interruption with only two dozen kB of memory.
It may surprise you, but we don’t talk about gigs or a mega.”
This is the first step in the development of new precipitation-imp-defiency technologies, a technology in which Nissan predicts use for mass-evidence cars in a decade.
The robot uses lithium polymer batteries, which are lighter and last longer than lithium-ion.
Another possible future use of this technology could be incorporating the unit into the GPS satellite navigation system, which would lead the driver to its destination, avoiding obstacles on the way.
