Morgan Stanley Opens Its MUFG Capital
The Banking House Morgan Stanley has announced that Mitsubishi UFJ Financial Group (MUFG), Japan's largest bank, which spends $9 billion, was entering nine-billion-dollar to gain 21 per cent of its capital. Adoption of this report was accompanied by a significant drop in its exchange rate.
A week after releasing its preliminary agreement, both banks explained that MUFG intends to buy 9.9 per cent of regular Morgan Stanley shares at an unified price of $25.25, a total of three-bli Billions.
The negotiated price is 19 % lower than the book value of the securities on 31 August.
MUFG is investing an additional $6 billion in acquiring convertential securities, which will provide it with a 10% yield and whose exchange rate is $31.25.
Morgan Stanley clarified that the whole operation will take place on the basis of an average uniform price of $29, i.e. 28 per cent below its current exchange rate.
However, Morgan Stanley shares lost 6.79% and reached $23.07 around 17.00 Greenwich time.
"This strategic merging represents an important opportunity on the path to accelerating Morgan Stanley's transition to the status of a banking holding company," explained John Mack, director-in-a-general of the U.S. group.
The new status was adopted by Morgan Stanley last week, which is intended to diversify its activities, but at the same time it puts it under the supervision of the Federal Reserve.
Compliance with the MUFG also confirms that today's financial crisis is forcing Wall Street's biggest aces to take a much more cautious approach than before.
The conditions presented on Monday show, among other things, that an agreement has been alternating over the last few days: last Monday Morgan only announced that it would sell up to 20% of its shares to Japan for about $8.5 billion.
"Just a week ago, we were thinking," Toshihide Mizuno, MUFG's chief executive, explained in Toki, "that our turnout will consist entirely of ordinary shares.
However, with regard to the current situation of economics and the market, we wanted to reduce the risks to a minimum and also cover ourselves."
Pursuance of a weighted average price, MUFG will pay 7% less for Morgan Stanley Securrencies.
And the operation will result in a reduction in the profit from the stock by about 20 percent.
An agreement presupposes that MUFG will in a year be able to confer half of its preferential securities into ordinary shares if the latter will be exchanged for a certain period of time at a rate of 50 per cent in excess of an exchange price, i.e. for about $47.
Morgan Stanley assures that MUFG's capital will bring will allow it to seize investment opportunities, strengthen its balance sheet, and increase its cash.
However, spokeswoman for the group declined to provide clarifying information on the strategic projects of the two new allies.
After MUFG's investment, Morgan Stanley is expected to boast Tier-One complacancy ratio of around 15%, i.e. one of the best-in-spirited.
Morgan clarified that since the end of mid-August "beneficial" he has reduced his balance sheet.
Recent weeks have been challenging for the group because its exchange rate has plummeted by mid-alphase, while insursulges have skyrocketed against the risk of non-payment associated with debt.
These costs gained the opposite development on Monday, but remained high, around 12 per cent.
Last week Goldman Sachs, the only "survivors" from Wall Street's big investment banks after the collapse of Bear Stearns and Lehman Brothers and Merrrill Lynch's buy-out that it had collected $15 billion, partly from Berkshire Hathaway, Warren Buffett.
Rafael Correa on Monday in Quito.
Relaunch a republic around the socialism of the 20th century.
Such is the joint project of Venezuelan Hugo Chavez, Bolivian Eva Morales and Ecuadorian Rafael Correy.
Last Sunday, the latter submitted a proposal for a new Constitution for a vote, following the example of his counterparts and friends.
And his mission is fulfilled, because 64% of the electorate, according to the official results covering 80% of the vote, voted "yes" in the referendum.
According to these results, it received 28%, 7% of votes were invalid and 0.7 per cent of the ballots were empty.
In order for the Constitution proposal to be adopted, he had to get half the vote, plus one.
Two polls carried out at the exit from the polling stations and the NGO's calculation foretold a clear "yes" victory on Sunday, situating them between 63% and 70 per cent.
Rafael Correa demanded the victories of the constitution on the night from Sunday to Monday.
"The insituation has prevailed all the way," he assured, congratulating the Guayaquil on the Guayaquil's Guuyaquil headquarters, 280 kilometers southwest of Quito.
It is self-evident that Rafael Correa received congratulations from Chavez and Morales, but also from his Chilean counterpart Michelle Bachelet.
Voting, which, according to the Monitoring Mission of the Organization of the United States of America (OEAs), took place peacefully concerned a text of 4,444 points building "new socialism" promoted by the president.
The proposal of the Constitution sanctifies the power of the state over the economy by preoccupidating the "planning of the development" of a country living primarily from the mining of diesel by foreign companies and the foreign exchange sent by its émigles.
A proposal sponsored by Correou, which was elected in 2006, also expands the powers of the head of state, which will be able to carry out two consecutive four-year mandates and will be able to dismantle the Chamber of Deputies.
Correa has already announced that it will take advantage of this power and will run for a new mandate in February 2009.
Among other things, the Constitution strengthens the principle of free health and education, which the president began to implement in a country of 13.9 million inhabitants, where 50% of the population lives below the poverty line.
Finally, it also envisages a ban on the presence of foreign troops, thereby ending an agreement between Washington and Quito on the use of an Ecuadorian base as the starting point of US anti-drug operations.
19 hostages have been freed in Chad
An Egyptian commando attacked the kidnappers of eleven hikers and their eight-member entourage, who were captured ten days ago.
They're in Cairo.
Eleven European hikers and their eight Egypsy guides who were held captive captive in the desert for ten days were taken to the Egyptian capital.
They were liberated by an Egyptian commando in Chad during the morning.
The hostages arrived around 15.30, apparently in good form, on board a special aircraft to the al-Maza military base, closely adjacent to the international airport in Cairo.
Immediately they were transported without the opportunity to express themselves in the press, aboard the helicopters to a hospital in Maadi, the outskirts of a residential neighborhood of Cairo to be subjected to medical examinations there.
"They are completely healthy" and "hijacks have not been disbursed by any ransoms," Egyptian Tourism Minister Zohejr Garrana said on the airwave.
Since its captive on September 19 during a safari in a four-wheel-drive vehicle at the foot of Mount Uanat, on the borders of Egypt, Sudan and Libya, a group of hostages have been dragged from one stash to the other with 35 unknown hijackers.
The group includes 11 hikers - five Italians, five Germans and one Romanian - as well as eight Egyptians: two guides, four drivers, one border headmistress of Aegyptous, an safari organizer.
A leading representative of the Egyptian security forces indicated that 30 members of special forces who approached the spot aboard two helicopters set off during the night just before dawn.
"A firefight has occurred, half the kidnappers were killed and the others flitted," said the source, who wished to remain anonymity.
Suggested that a total of 150 members of the Egyptian special forces from the "flash" unit were sent to Sudan, where they gained the support of German and Italian commandos.
The head of Italian diplomacy Franco Frattini from Belgrade, where he is currently located, he mentioned the role played by secret agents and the components of the Italian special forces.
"We have to admit that we have achieved this result thanks to the professionalism and efficiencies of the people from our secret services, special forces," he said without further clarification.
He also deniebrated that a ransom had been dispensed.
On the eve, the U.S. military said it detained a four-wheel-propelled vehicle during the patrol, on which the hijackers were located, killing six, including the Chadian commander and arresting two.
The army argued that they were connected to a group of rebels in Darfur, the Movement for Liberation of Sudan - Unity (SLA-U), which the movement immediately declined.
